# Command pgxcli

## What does this command do:

Postgres CLI with autocompletion and syntax highlighting

## Installed from

For Fedora install using: curl -sSfL https://github.com/Balaji01-4D/pgxcli/releases/download/v0.1.0/pgxcli_0.1.0_linux_amd64.rpm -o /tmp/pgxcli.rpm && sudo dnf install -y /tmp/pgxcli.rpm
For Raspberry Pi install using: curl -sSfL https://github.com/Balaji01-4D/pgxcli/releases/download/v0.1.0/pgxcli_0.1.0_linux_arm64.tar.gz | tar -xzf - -C /tmp && mv /tmp/pgxcli ~/.local/bin/pgxcli && chmod +x ~/.local/bin/pgxcli
For Mac install using: curl -sSfL https://github.com/Balaji01-4D/pgxcli/releases/download/v0.1.0/pgxcli_0.1.0_darwin_arm64.tar.gz | tar -xzf - -C /tmp && mv /tmp/pgxcli ~/.local/bin/pgxcli && chmod +x ~/.local/bin/pgxcli

## Command path

For Fedora run: /usr/bin/pgxcli
For Raspberry Pi run: ~/.local/bin/pgxcli
For Mac run: ~/.local/bin/pgxcli

## Command documentation

Docs can be found at https://github.com/Balaji01-4D/pgxcli

## Command options

# Connect by positional args (dbname, username)
pgxcli mydb myuser

# Connect by flags
pgxcli --host localhost --port 5432 --user postgres --dbname postgres

# Connect via URI
pgxcli postgres://user:password@localhost:5432/dbname

# Launch interactive connection form
pgxcli -i

# Flags
#   -d, --dbname string     database name to connect to
#       --debug             enable debug mode for verbose logging
#   -h, --host string       host address of the postgres database
#   -i, --interactive       interactive connection mode
#   -w, --no-password       never prompt for the password
#   -W, --password          force password prompt
#   -p, --port uint16       port number (default 5432)
#   -U, --user string       username
#   -v, --version           print version

# Useful backslash commands inside the REPL
#   \l    list databases
#   \dt   list tables
#   \d    describe a table
#   \q    quit
